    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d822">

                <pb facs="0880" n="870" sameAs="frus:frus1949v09/pg870"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">693.419/8–2449</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Memorandum by Mr. Livingston T.
                            Merchant of the Office of Far Eastern Affairs to the Chief of
                        the Division of Chinese Affairs</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">Sprouse</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">[<placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>,] <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1949-08-24">August 24, 1949</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>Mr. Rusk<anchor n="59" xml:id="d822-f1"/> indicated to me this afternoon that the
                    President is moving rapidly toward an attitude and policy with respect to
                    Communist China which will result in an abandonment of the thesis <pb
                        facs="0881" n="871" xml:id="pg871"/> contained in NSC 41.<anchor n="60"
                        xml:id="d822-f2"/> It apparently is still his desire to prevent the American
                    tankers from delivering oil to Manchuria.<anchor n="61" xml:id="d822-f3"/> We
                    are also immediately instructed to explore in more concrete terms the
                    possibility of aid to Li and Pai.<anchor n="62" xml:id="d822-f4"/> In this
                    connection I think you should attempt urgently to reach Kavanaugh of CalTex and
                    ask him to drag his feet on the fuel oil contract for Shanghai.</p>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d822-f1" n="59" xml:id="d822-f1-content">
                <p>Dean Rusk, Deputy Under Secretary of State.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d822-f2" n="60" xml:id="d822-f2-content">
                <p>February 28, <ref target="#pg826">p. 826</ref>.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d822-f3" n="61" xml:id="d822-f3-content">
                <p>For correspondence on this subject, see <ref target="#pg1002">pp. 1002</ref>
                    ff.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d822-f4" n="62" xml:id="d822-f4-content">
                <p>Marshal Li Tsung-jen was Acting President of the Republic of China, and General
                    Pai Chung-hsi was his principal military supporter. For correspondence on
                    military aid, see <ref target="#pg472">pp. 472</ref> ff.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
